Q: Print the whole linked list in gdb? A: <p>Following the answer by @n-m-is-an-unemployed-ai-agent, you can use GDB pretty printers to see contents of a linked list. This is useful also because it works fine with modern IDEs (VS Code, CLion, etc).</p>
<hr />
<p>Put this into a file <code>pp.py</code>:</p>
<pre class="lang-py prettyprint-override"><code>import gdb

# generic utility to pretty-print a linked list
# it terminates on NULL pointer or if it detects a cycle
def linked_list_children_list(first_node, func_next_node, func_item_of_node = None):
    if not func_item_of_node:
        func_item_of_node = lambda p: p
    pnode = first_node
    visited_addresses = set()
    k = 0
    while pnode and int(pnode) != 0:
        name = '[%d]' % k
        cycled = int(pnode) in visited_addresses
        if cycled:
            name = '[cycle]'
        yield (name, func_item_of_node(pnode))
        k += 1
        visited_addresses.add(int(pnode))
        if cycled:
            break
        pnode = func_next_node(pnode)

#=========================================================================

# pretty printer specifically for your data structure
class ListPrinter:
    def __init__(self, value):
        self.value = value

    def to_string(self):
        return 'List[%d]' % int(self.value['size'])

    def children(self):
        # the lambdas that we pass here specify how to traverse the list
        return linked_list_children_list(
            self.value['head'],
            lambda x: x.dereference()['next'],
            lambda x: x.dereference()['data']
        )

# unfortunately, GDB does not automatically match pointer type
# if value type is assigned to a pretty printer
# so if you want to inspect variables of type 'list_t *',
# you also have to make and register a pretty printer for pointer =(
class ListPtrPrinter:
    def __init__(self, value):
        self.value = value
        self.printer = ListPrinter(value)
    def to_string(self):
        return '0x%x ' % int(self.value) + self.printer.to_string()
    def children(self):
        return self.printer.children()

# here I use old-style registration, because the new one does not work with pointers
def ppmatcher(value):
    if str(value.type) in ['list', 'list_t']:
        return ListPrinter(value)
    if str(value.type) in ['list *', 'list_t *']:
        return ListPtrPrinter(value)
    return None

if ppmatcher in gdb.pretty_printers:
    del gdb.pretty_printers[gdb.pretty_printers.index(ppmatcher)]
gdb.pretty_printers.append(ppmatcher)
</code></pre>
<p>And I'll use this C file for testing:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>#include &lt;stdint.h&gt;
#include &lt;stddef.h&gt;

typedef int data_t;
typedef struct node node_t;

struct node {
    data_t data;
    node_t *next;
};

typedef struct list {
    node_t *head;
    node_t *foot;
    node_t *curr;   // for iterator
    unsigned int size;
} list_t;

int main() {
    #define NUM 10
    node_t nodes[NUM];
    int last = -1;
    for (int i = 0; i &lt; NUM; i++) {
        nodes[i].data = i * 111;
        nodes[i].next = &amp;nodes[(i + 3) % NUM];
        if (nodes[i].next == &amp;nodes[0])
            last = i;
    }
    nodes[last].next = NULL;
    list_t head;
    head.head = &amp;nodes[0];
    head.foot = &amp;nodes[last];
    head.curr = NULL;
    head.size = NUM;

    list_t* myList = &amp;head;
    return 0;
}
</code></pre>
<p>Now to make it work:</p>
<ol>
<li>Build C code.</li>
<li>Run executable under GDB until <code>return 0</code>.</li>
<li>Execute <code>source pp.py</code> in GDB console.</li>
<li>Execute <code>print myList</code> or inspect <code>myList</code> in your IDE.</li>
</ol>
<p>Here is the printout from GDB:</p>
<pre><code>$2 = 0x7fffffffd370 List[10] = {[0] = 0, [1] = 333, [2] = 666, [3] = 999, [4] = 222, [5] = 555, [6] = 888, [7] = 111, [8] = 444, [9] = 777}
</code></pre>
<p>And in VS Code it has value <code>0x7fffffffd370 List[10]</code>, and shows 10 integer numbers as children when expanded.</p>
<hr />
<p>The generic code for linked list pretty printers comes form here:</p>
<ul>
<li><a href="https://dirtyhandscoding.github.io/posts/natvis-like-experience-with-gdb-pretty-printers-implementing-natvis-features.html#arrays-and-lists" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://dirtyhandscoding.github.io/posts/natvis-like-experience-with-gdb-pretty-printers-implementing-natvis-features.html#arrays-and-lists</a></li>
</ul>
<p>The issue with pointers (having to write many trivial pretty printers for pointer types) is a very messy one, unfortunately. I have mentioned it <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/questions/71906297/full-gdb-pretty-printer-class-syntax-for-simple-c-class-example/79989886#79989886">here</a>, and the article discusses it in great detail...</p>
